<setup>
  <selectFromSequence name="a" from="2" to="4" step="1"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="k" from="2" to="5" step="1" exclude="$a"/>

  <function name="fgenTerm" variables="n">n(x-$a)^n/$k^n</function>
</setup>

<p>
  Use the ratio test to determine the open interval of convergence for the series, and report your result in interval notation in the space provided below.
</p>

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
        For a function <m>f</m> whose Taylor series is
        <md>
          <mrow>
          T_f(x) \amp= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{n(x-$a)^n}{$k^n}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= \frac{x-$a}{$k} + \frac{2(x-$a)^2}{$k^2} + \frac{3(x-$a)^3}{$k^3} + \cdots
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Let <m>r_n(x)</m> be the ratio of the <m>(n+1)^{\text{st}}</m> term to the <m>n^{\text{th}} </m> term. Enter the simplest expression you can for <m>r_n(x)</m>, making sure your answer is a single fractional expression.
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer name="fratio" symbolicEquality simplifyOnCompare="normalizeOrder"><label><m>r_n(x)=</m></label>(n+1)(x-$a)/($k*n)</answer>
      </p>
      <!-- <feedback condition="$fratio = <math expandonCompare>(n+1)(x-$a)/($k*n)</math> and $fratio.responseHasBeenSubmitted and $fratio.creditAchieved!=1">You are close, but enter your answer as a single fraction in the simplest terms, and do not multiply terms out. </feedback> had trouble getting this to work, gave up for now. -->
      <p>
        Let <m>\displaystyle r(x)=\lim_{n\to\infty}r_n(x)</m>.  Evaluate this limit.
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer symbolicEquality simplifyOnCompare="full"><label><m>r(x)=</m></label>(x-$a)/($k)</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><label>The open interval of convergence is: </label><interval>(-$k+$a, $k+$a)</interval></answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        For a function <m>g</m> whose Taylor series is
        <md>
          <mrow>
          T_g(x) \amp= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{$a^{2n}(x+$k)^n}{n^2}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= $a^2(x+$k) + \frac{$a^4(x+$k)^2}{4} + \frac{$a^6(x+$k)^3}{9} + \cdots
            </mrow>
        </md>
        Let <m>r_n(x)</m> be the ratio of the <m>(n+1)^{\text{st}}</m> term to the <m>n^{\text{th}} </m> term. Enter the simplest expression you can for <m>r_n(x)</m>, making sure your answer is a single fractional expression.
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer symbolicEquality simplifyOnCompare="normalizeOrder"><label><m>r_n(x)=</m></label>
        <award>$a^2*n^2(x+$k)/(n+1)^2</award>
        <award><number>$a^2</number>*n^2(x+$k)/(n+1)^2</award></answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        Let <m>\displaystyle r(x)=\lim_{n\to\infty}r_n(x)</m>.  Evaluate this limit.
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer symbolicEquality simplifyOnCompare="full"><label><m>r(x)=</m></label>$a^2*(x+$k)</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer allowedErrorInNumbers=".001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><label>The open interval of convergence is: </label><interval>(-$k-1/$a^2, -$k+1/$a^2)</interval></answer>
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>